  "query_preview": "..The forecast area remains sandwiched between an area\nof high pressure to the east and a broad area of low pressure off\nto the west, with shortwave energy continuing to lift through the\nregion this evening. The pairing of the shortwave energy and the\nmonsoonal moisture on the western periphery of the ridge continues\nto support shower and thunderstorm activity across the region,\nwith a rather widespread area of stratiform rain across a portion\nof northern Utah. Isolated thunderstorms continue to build in\nwest-central and southwest Utah this evening, with isolated to\nwidely scattered storms expected to continue into the early\nmorning hours across this same region. As such, the threat for\nheavy rainfall and flash flooding will remain somewhat elevated\nthrough early Friday morning, with primary concern areas being the\nCottonwood burn scar and Widemouth 2 scar. \n\nMoisture remains in place through the day tomorrow with another\nsubtle shortwave trough lifting through the region which will help\nto bring another round of showers and thunderstorms across the\nforecast area. As has been the case for the past several days, the\nincrease in atmospheric moisture and instability in place across\nthe region will bring the potential for heavy rainfall and flash\nflooding with developing thunderstorms if they end up in the wrong\nplace (i.e. burn scars, slot canyons, dry washes, and poor\ndrainage / urbanized areas).\n\nIncreasing subsidence in the wake of this shortwave will spread\nover the region over the weekend, helping to decrease the overall\ncoverage of showers and thunderstorms areawide. That said,\nmoisture will still remain in place and allow for at least\nisolated showers and thunderstorms to develop over the high\nterrain features, particularly in southern Utah and in the Uinta\nMountains. Coverage of precipitation will decrease further into\nMonday and Tuesday as mid-level moisture wanes, however, we'll\nincrease chances once again on Wednesday as the northern stream\nbecomes more active.",
